The examples in this directory are described in the userguide.

Short description
-----------------

startup1:

 Startup from zero stress of flow with a constant velocity gradient
 Simple shear flow
 The model is a Hookean dumbbell (stochastic model)
 The integration scheme is explicit Euler

startup2:

 Stochastic problem on a unit square
 linear Couette flow
 DEVSS/DG
 Hookean dumbbell. Initial disturbance of the velocity to generate a
 disturbance in the Q-vectors.
 BCF explicit first-order scheme

startup3:

 Startup from zero stress of flow with a constant velocity gradient
 Uniaxial elongational flow
 The model is a FENE model
 The integration scheme is explicit Euler

startup4:

 Startup from zero stress of flow with a constant velocity gradient
 Simple shear flow
 The model is a Hookean dumbbell (stochastic model)
 The integration scheme is implicit Euler

startup5:

 Startup from zero stress of flow with a constant velocity gradient
 Planar elongational flow
 The model is a FENE model
 The integration scheme is explicit Euler

startup6:

 Startup from zero stress of flow with a constant velocity gradient
 Uniaxial elongational flow
 The model is a FENE model
 The integration scheme is implicit Euler

couette1:

 Stochastic problem on a unit square
 linear Couette flow
 DEVSS/DG
 Hookean dumbbell. Initial disturbance of the velocity to generate a
 disturbance in the Q-vectors.
 BCF explicit first-order scheme

cylinder1:

 Flow around a cylinder confined between two plates for a Hookean dumbbell,
 solved using BCF. The entry and exit sides are connected by periodical
 boundary conditions and a constant flow rate is imposed.
 Only the upper-half of the domain is modelled and symmetry conditions are
 assumed (zero tractions).
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 DEVSS/DG
 first-order time integration

cylinder2:

 Flow around a cylinder confined between two plates for a Hookean dumbbell,
 solved using BCF. The entry and exit sides are connected by periodical
 boundary conditions and a constant flow rate is imposed.
 Only the upper-half of the domain is modelled and symmetry conditions are
 assumed (zero tractions).
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 DEVSS/DG
 first-order time integration

 This problem is similar to cylinder1, however it shows in addition the
 possibilities to output the determinant of c in three different ways:
   1) compute detc in the element nodes and average element contributions
   2) compute detc in the element nodes and store elementwise (no averaging)
   3) compute detc in the Gauss points

cylinder3:

 Flow around a cylinder confined between two plates for a FENE dumbbell,
 solved using BCF. The entry and exit sides are connected by periodical
 boundary conditions and a constant flow rate is imposed.
 Only the upper-half of the domain is modelled and symmetry conditions are
 assumed (zero tractions). 
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 DEVSS/DG
 first-order implicit time integration

streamfunction:

 streamfunction problem for the flow around a cylinder
